  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C6.S2 — Phase kickback and the Hadamard test</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C6.S2 — One qubit of phase information</p></div><p>Let \(U|u\rangle=e^{2\pi i\varphi}|u\rangle\). Prepare the control in \(|+\rangle\), apply controlled-\(U\), then a Hadamard on the control, then measure it.</p><p>(a) Give \(\Pr[0]\) as a simplified function of <code>phi</code>.</p><p>\(\Pr[0]=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Give \(\Pr[0]-\Pr[1]\), again as a function of <code>phi</code>.</p><p>\(\Pr[0]-\Pr[1]=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>After the controlled operation the control is \(\frac{1}{\sqrt2}(|0\rangle+e^{2\pi i\varphi}|1\rangle)\) and the target is untouched — this is phase kickback. The Hadamard turns it into \(\frac{1+e^{2\pi i\varphi}}{2}|0\rangle+\frac{1-e^{2\pi i\varphi}}{2}|1\rangle\).</p><p>(a) \(\Pr[0]=\left|\frac{1+e^{2\pi i\varphi}}{2}\right|^{2}=\cos^{2}(\pi\varphi)\).</p><p>(b) \(\Pr[0]-\Pr[1]=\cos(2\pi\varphi)=\mathrm{Re}\,\langle u|U|u\rangle\).</p><p>Each shot therefore gives one Bernoulli sample of a quantity whose mean encodes the phase. Estimating \(\varphi\) this way costs \(O(1/\varepsilon^{2})\) shots; full QPE costs \(O(1/\varepsilon)\) applications of \(U\), the Heisenberg-limited scaling.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
